{"generation_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d","teaching_plan":{"arc":"This lesson opens on a seesaw in a playground, where one child pushes down and the other end rises, and asks what the plank is tilting around. It pins the three parts of a lever — fulcrum, effort, load — to actual positions on that seesaw, then sets that model beside the two beliefs it is most likely to meet: that the pushing end is the fulcrum, and that only made tools like crowbars count. It closes by having the learner read the three parts off a lever they have not seen before.","revision":1,"sections":[{"blocks":[{"id":"orient-b1","brief":"Present one child pushing down on one end of a seesaw while the other end with the second child rises. Ask what the plank is tilting around, and let the learner notice that it only moves because something in the middle stays still and supports it. Do not name the fulcrum, the effort, or the load yet, and do not explain the mechanism.","intent":"orient","evidence":"The objective requires locating three parts on a real lever, so the learner must first hold a concrete seesaw that moves; naming a part before the movement is seen would leave the term with nothing to attach to.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["anchor.anchor-1","lesson.objective","slot.orient.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"orient","transition":null,"specific_purpose":"Open attention on the seesaw and establish a reason to find out what makes it work."},{"blocks":[{"id":"explain-b1","brief":"State that a lever has exactly three parts, and place each on the seesaw. The fulcrum is the central support the plank balances on. The effort is the downward push the first child makes on one end. The load is the child on the other end who is being lifted. Make clear that the three parts are three different places on the seesaw, not three names for the push. Do not yet say what happens when the pushing end is mistaken for the support.","intent":"name-parts","evidence":"The objective asks the learner to name fulcrum, effort, and load, which is a parts-identification job rather than a causal one; naming them on the seesaw gives each term a fixed physical referent before any contrast is introduced.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","anchor.anchor-1","slot.explain.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]},{"id":"explain-b2","brief":"Define a lever as a rigid bar that tilts around a single fixed point, with the push applied at one place and the thing being moved at another. Distinguish the fulcrum from the effort and the load: the fulcrum is the point that does not move, while the effort and the load are the two ends that do. Do not discuss specific instruments or which objects qualify.","intent":"define","evidence":"Prior lessons gave the learner a push-or-pull notion of force and a simple-machine notion of tools that make work easier, so the definition must connect those by fixing the fulcrum as the point that stays still while the push is applied elsewhere.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","prior-1","prior-2","anchor.anchor-1"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"explain","transition":"The learner has a seesaw that tilts but no names for what it is doing, so this section supplies the names and attaches each one to a spot on that seesaw.","specific_purpose":"Build the three-part model of a lever and pin each part to a real position on the seesaw."},{"blocks":[{"id":"contrast-b1","brief":"Put the correct reading beside the wrong one on the same seesaw. Correct: the fulcrum is the central support the plank balances on. Wrong: the fulcrum is the end where the first child pushes down, because that is where the action happens. Show that if the fulcrum were the pushing end, then the point the plank actually pivots around would be unnamed, and the plank would have to be tilting around a place where nothing is touching. Do not re-explain what the effort and load are.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"M1 and M2 both relocate the fulcrum to the place where force is applied, so the belief can only be broken by showing that the pushing end is not the point of balance; this is a held model to be surfaced, not a slip to flag.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["M1","M2","anchor.anchor-1","slot.contrast.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":"diagnose-misconception is atypical for this slot: the slot's typical intents contrast two legitimate alternatives, but here one side is a held wrong belief about where the fulcrum is, which must be surfaced and tested rather than merely distinguished.","source_question_ids":["8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i2"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]},{"id":"contrast-b2","brief":"Give the rule that makes something a lever: a rigid bar that tilts around a fixed point, with the push applied away from that point and the thing being moved on the other side. Sort by that rule: a seesaw, a bottle opener, and a crowbar all satisfy it and all have a fulcrum, an effort, and a load; a wheel rolling in a straight line does not tilt around a fixed point and is not a lever. Include the seesaw explicitly so it is not treated as an exception.","intent":"classify","evidence":"M3 denies that everyday objects count as levers, and prior-3 established that everyday objects can be described by how they are used, so a membership rule that puts the seesaw, bottle opener, and crowbar in one category draws exactly the boundary the learner needs.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["M3","prior-3","lesson.objective","slot.contrast.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":["8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i3"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"contrast","transition":"The learner now holds a named fulcrum, effort, and load on the seesaw, so this section tests that model against the two wrong beliefs it is most likely to meet.","specific_purpose":"Set the three-part model beside the two beliefs that would misplace or deny it."},{"blocks":[{"id":"check-b1","brief":"Have the learner identify the fulcrum, the effort, and the load on a lever they have not seen before, and name which part is which in that new case. The case must not be the seesaw. Correct reading means all three parts are assigned to the right places, not just that the words are recalled.","intent":"check-understanding","evidence":"The objective is identification of parts, and a fresh lever is the only way to tell whether the learner can locate the fulcrum, effort, and load rather than recognising the seesaw they were taught on.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","slot.check.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":["8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i1"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"check","transition":"The learner has the three-part model and has seen it survive the two wrong beliefs, so this section checks whether it transfers to an unfamiliar lever.","specific_purpose":"Have the learner read the three parts off a lever they have not seen, so the objective is measured rather than rehearsed."}],"anchor_usage":[{"usage":"Open on the seesaw with one child pushing down on one end and the other end rising, and ask what the plank is tilting around — this is the reason to care.","slot_id":"orient"},{"usage":"Label the seesaw itself: the central support the plank balances on is the fulcrum, the downward push on one end is the effort, the child on the other end is the load.","slot_id":"explain"},{"usage":"The seesaw holds the correct model while two near-misses are placed beside it: pointing at the pushing end and calling it the fulcrum, and claiming the seesaw does not count as a lever because it is not a made tool.","slot_id":"contrast"},{"usage":"The learner reads the three parts off a lever they have not seen before, so the seesaw must not be the case being diagnosed.","slot_id":"check"}],"approval_status":"approved","preparation_hash":"a9530874d702efc409dcd4c87658fe037981b8a8a6ed76e1d95a551ff8885870","teaching_plan_id":"d5fc5404-f964-4100-b1ce-007cdd996f90","misconception_focus_ids":["M1","M3"]},"teaching_validation":{"ok":true,"issues":[]},"teaching_qc":[],"teaching_review":{"status":"approved","revision":2,"reviewed_at":"2026-09-11T03:42:21.791918+00:00","reviewed_by":"2f297058-f6bc-47bf-9ffd-3208ad8246d4","teacher_note":"Approved for Print","approved_revision":1},"lesson_packet":{"scope":{"terminology":[],"must_establish":[],"must_not_introduce":[]},"slots":[{"purpose":"Give the learner a reason to care and a concrete situation to hold.","slot_id":"orient","max_blocks":2,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["orient","activate-prior-knowledge","emphasise"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"Build the idea itself. State it, ground it, make it usable.","slot_id":"explain","max_blocks":3,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["explain-cause","explain","define","show-structure","trace-flow","compare","name-parts"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"Put a case and a near-miss side by side. The learner must see the boundary of the idea, not just the idea.\n","slot_id":"contrast","max_blocks":3,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["compare","classify","explain","warn","show-structure"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"The shared diagnostic. Identical across every variant. Written from the card only, never from the prose above it.\n","slot_id":"check","max_blocks":1,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["check-understanding","practise-independent","diagnose-misconception"],"visual_required":false}],"anchor":{"id":"anchor-1","description":"A seesaw in a playground: a plank balanced on a central support, one child pushing down on one end…"},"lesson":{"subject":"Science","objective":"Identify a lever in everyday life and name its fulcrum, effort, and load","grade_level":"Grade","lesson_mode":"first_exposure","knowledge_type":"conceptual","path_lesson_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d"},"limits":{"max_sections":4,"max_total_blocks":10,"max_blocks_per_section":3},"resource_id":"lesson","approved_items":[{"id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i1","stem":"A student presses down on one end of a seesaw while a friend sits on the other end. The raised bar pivots on a fixed support in the middle. Which part of this seesaw is the fulcrum?","card_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The fixed support in the middle where the bar pivots","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"The end where the student presses down","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"The place where the friend's body touches the bar","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"The entire raised bar between both ends","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M1","c":"M2","d":null},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i2","stem":"A person uses a bottle opener to lift the metal cap off a glass bottle. The opener rests on the bottle's rim and pulls up on the cap when the handle is pressed. In this action, what is the fulcrum?","card_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The point where the opener rests on the bottle's rim","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"The handle of the opener that is pressed down","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"The metal cap that is lifted off","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"The entire bottle opener, since it is one solid tool","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M1","c":"M2","d":"M3"},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i3","stem":"A child sits on one end of a seesaw. A teenager pushes down on the other end to raise the child. What are the effort and the load in this action?","card_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Effort: the teenager pushing down; load: the child being raised","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"Effort: the child sitting on the seesaw; load: the teenager pushing down","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"Effort: the central support; load: the child being raised","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"Effort: the whole seesaw; load: the child being raised","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M2","c":"M1","d":null},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i4","stem":"A gardener pushes down on the handle of a wheelbarrow to lift a heavy bag of soil. The wheelbarrow pivots on its wheel. Which statement correctly identifies the lever parts?","card_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Fulcrum: the wheel; effort: the gardener's push on the handle; load: the bag of soil","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"Fulcrum: the handle; effort: the gardener's push; load: the wheel","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"Fulcrum: the bag of soil; effort: the gardener's push; load: the wheel","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"Fulcrum: the wheel; effort: the bag of soil; load: the gardener's push","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M1","c":"M2","d":null},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160.i5","stem":"Which of these everyday actions uses a lever, and what is its fulcrum?","card_id":"8b855594-9577-44f1-8652-cf2a4dae699d:219cc61d-a2cb-48c5-9638-554b63824160","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Using a crowbar to pry up a nail; fulcrum is the block or surface it rests on","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"Sitting on a seesaw; fulcrum is the end where a person sits","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"Carrying a book across a room; fulcrum is the person's hand","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"Squeezing a sponge under water; fulcrum is the sponge itself","correct":false,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M1","c":"M3","d":null},"correct_key":"a"}],"misconceptions":[{"id":"M1","statement":"{'id': 'M1', 'description': 'The end of the lever where you push or pull is the fulcrum.', 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"M2","statement":"{'id': 'M2', 'description': 'Anything you push or pull is itself the fulcrum, because that is where the action happens.', 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"M3","statement":"{'id': 'M3', 'description': 'Only specially made machines like crowbars count as levers; everyday objects like seesaws or bottle openers do not.', 'source': 'drafted'}"}],"prio